§§ `Rule 3 -- satisfaction by consent`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:3441 | 3. —(1) Any person who has satisfied a judgment debt may on filing a consent of the judgment creditor in Form 39 apply to the Court for satisfaction to be recorded, and the Court may order satisfaction to be recorded accordingly. (2) The consent of the judgment creditor must be attested by the judgment creditor’s solicitor or if the judgment creditor has no solicitor, by a commissioner for oaths.
@ref O 22 r 3(1) (src:3441-3441)
GIVEN `the person has satisfied a judgment debt` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has filed the consent of the judgment creditor in Form 39` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 22 r 3(1) the person may apply to the Court for satisfaction to be recorded` MEANS
    IF `the person has satisfied a judgment debt` AND `the person has filed the consent of the judgment creditor in Form 39`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Any person who has satisfied a judgment debt may on filing a consent of the judgment creditor in Form 39 apply to the Court for satisfaction to be recorded"

-- src:3441 | 3. —(1) Any person who has satisfied a judgment debt may on filing a consent of the judgment creditor in Form 39 apply to the Court for satisfaction to be recorded, and the Court may order satisfaction to be recorded accordingly. (2) The consent of the judgment creditor must be attested by the judgment creditor’s solicitor or if the judgment creditor has no solicitor, by a commissioner for oaths.
@ref O 22 r 3(1) (src:3441-3441)
GIVEN `the person has satisfied a judgment debt` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has filed the consent of the judgment creditor in Form 39` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has applied to the Court for satisfaction to be recorded` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 22 r 3(1) the Court may order satisfaction to be recorded` MEANS
    IF `the person has satisfied a judgment debt` AND `the person has filed the consent of the judgment creditor in Form 39` AND `the person has applied to the Court for satisfaction to be recorded`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "the Court may order satisfaction to be recorded accordingly"

-- src:3441 | 3. —(1) Any person who has satisfied a judgment debt may on filing a consent of the judgment creditor in Form 39 apply to the Court for satisfaction to be recorded, and the Court may order satisfaction to be recorded accordingly. (2) The consent of the judgment creditor must be attested by the judgment creditor’s solicitor or if the judgment creditor has no solicitor, by a commissioner for oaths.
@ref O 22 r 3(2) (src:3441-3441)
GIVEN `the judgment creditor has a solicitor` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the consent is attested by the judgment creditor's solicitor` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the consent is attested by a commissioner for oaths` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 22 r 3(2) the consent is attested as the rule requires` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the judgment creditor has a solicitor` AND `the consent is attested by the judgment creditor's solicitor`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `the judgment creditor has a solicitor`
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The consent of the judgment creditor must be attested by the judgment creditor’s solicitor"
        IF `the consent is attested by a commissioner for oaths`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "or if the judgment creditor has no solicitor, by a commissioner for oaths"

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 4 -- discharge of a non-party`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:3443 | 4. Any payment made by a non‑party in compliance with an enforcement order for attachment of debt under Rule 2(2)( c ), and any execution of an enforcement order against that person pursuant to such an order, is a valid discharge of that person’s liability to the enforcement respondent to the extent of the amount paid or realised even if the proceedings for the enforcement order for attachment of debt are subsequently set aside or the judgment or order from which they arose reversed.
@ref O 22 r 4 (src:3443-3443)
GIVEN `the payment was made by a non-party in compliance with an enforcement order for attachment of debt under Rule 2(2)(c), or the enforcement order was executed against the non-party pursuant to such an order` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 22 r 4 the payment or execution is a valid discharge of the non-party's liability to the enforcement respondent` MEANS
    IF `the payment was made by a non-party in compliance with an enforcement order for attachment of debt under Rule 2(2)(c), or the enforcement order was executed against the non-party pursuant to such an order`
    THEN JUST TRUE -- the text adds "even if the proceedings for the enforcement order for attachment of debt are subsequently set aside or the judgment or order from which they arose reversed", so a later setting aside or reversal does not change the answer
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Any payment made by a non‑party in compliance with an enforcement order for attachment of debt under Rule 2(2)( c ), and any execution of an enforcement order against that person pursuant to such an order"

-- "to the extent of the amount paid or realised".
-- src:3443 | 4. Any payment made by a non‑party in compliance with an enforcement order for attachment of debt under Rule 2(2)( c ), and any execution of an enforcement order against that person pursuant to such an order, is a valid discharge of that person’s liability to the enforcement respondent to the extent of the amount paid or realised even if the proceedings for the enforcement order for attachment of debt are subsequently set aside or the judgment or order from which they arose reversed.
@ref O 22 r 4 (src:3443-3443)
GIVEN `the payment was made by a non-party in compliance with an enforcement order for attachment of debt under Rule 2(2)(c), or the enforcement order was executed against the non-party pursuant to such an order` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the amount paid or realised` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`o 22 r 4 the extent of the discharge` MEANS
    IF `o 22 r 4 the payment or execution is a valid discharge of the non-party's liability to the enforcement respondent` `the payment was made by a non-party in compliance with an enforcement order for attachment of debt under Rule 2(2)(c), or the enforcement order was executed against the non-party pursuant to such an order` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN JUST `the amount paid or realised`
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "to the extent of the amount paid or realised"

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 5 -- money in Court`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (1): where money stands to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court, no enforcement order for attachment of
-- debt in respect of that money, but the applicant may apply by summons for payment.
-- src:3445 | 5. —(1) Where money is standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court, the enforcement applicant is not entitled to an enforcement order for attachment of debt in respect of that money but may apply to the Court by summons for an order that the money or so much of the money as is sufficient to satisfy the judgment or order sought to be enforced and the costs of the application be paid to the enforcement applicant. (2) On issuing a summons under this Rule, the applicant must produce the summons at the office of the Accountant‑General and leave a copy at that office, and the money to which the application relates must not be paid out of Court until after the determination of the application.
@ref O 22 r 5(1) (src:3445-3445)
GIVEN `money is standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 22 r 5(1) the enforcement applicant is entitled to an enforcement order for attachment of debt in respect of the money` MEANS
    IF `money is standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court`
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the enforcement applicant is not entitled to an enforcement order for attachment of debt in respect of that money"
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Where money is standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court"

-- src:3445 | 5. —(1) Where money is standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court, the enforcement applicant is not entitled to an enforcement order for attachment of debt in respect of that money but may apply to the Court by summons for an order that the money or so much of the money as is sufficient to satisfy the judgment or order sought to be enforced and the costs of the application be paid to the enforcement applicant. (2) On issuing a summons under this Rule, the applicant must produce the summons at the office of the Accountant‑General and leave a copy at that office, and the money to which the application relates must not be paid out of Court until after the determination of the application.
@ref O 22 r 5(1) (src:3445-3445)
GIVEN `money is standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 22 r 5(1) the enforcement applicant may apply to the Court by summons for payment of the money to the enforcement applicant` MEANS
    IF `money is standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Where money is standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court"

-- "the money or so much of the money as is sufficient to satisfy the judgment or order sought to be enforced and the
-- costs of the application".
-- src:3445 | 5. —(1) Where money is standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court, the enforcement applicant is not entitled to an enforcement order for attachment of debt in respect of that money but may apply to the Court by summons for an order that the money or so much of the money as is sufficient to satisfy the judgment or order sought to be enforced and the costs of the application be paid to the enforcement applicant. (2) On issuing a summons under this Rule, the applicant must produce the summons at the office of the Accountant‑General and leave a copy at that office, and the money to which the application relates must not be paid out of Court until after the determination of the application.
@ref O 22 r 5(1) (src:3445-3445)
GIVEN `money is standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the money standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court` IS A NUMBER
      `the sum needed to satisfy the judgment or order sought to be enforced` IS A NUMBER
      `the costs of the application` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`o 22 r 5(1) the most that may be ordered to be paid to the enforcement applicant` MEANS
    IF `money is standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court`
    THEN JUST (min `the money standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court` (`the sum needed to satisfy the judgment or order sought to be enforced` PLUS `the costs of the application`))
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Where money is standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court"

-- (2): on issuing a summons, produce it at the office of the Accountant-General and leave a copy; no payment out
-- until after the determination of the application (an "until" bar, ruled 2026-11-05 (d)).
-- src:3445 | 5. —(1) Where money is standing to the credit of the enforcement respondent in Court, the enforcement applicant is not entitled to an enforcement order for attachment of debt in respect of that money but may apply to the Court by summons for an order that the money or so much of the money as is sufficient to satisfy the judgment or order sought to be enforced and the costs of the application be paid to the enforcement applicant. (2) On issuing a summons under this Rule, the applicant must produce the summons at the office of the Accountant‑General and leave a copy at that office, and the money to which the application relates must not be paid out of Court until after the determination of the application.
@ref O 22 r 5(2) (src:3445-3445)
